WHAT IS ORIGINAL HERE

All of it. There is no libc taken from elsewhere (the one in sdk/libc was
written here), no bootloader borrowed from elsewhere, no driver lifted from
another kernel, no cryptography library, no font file, no image file. The TCP/IP stack, the TLS 1.3 implementation, the certificate
checking, the FAT driver, the window server, the web browser, the JavaScript
interpreter, both bootloaders and every device driver were written for this
project from their specifications.

Where a published document was implemented, it is named in the source at the
point of implementation: RFC 7748 for X25519, RFC 8448 for the TLS key
schedule, FIPS 180 for the hashes, the NIST vectors for AES-GCM, and so on.
Those are specifications that were read, not code that was copied.

The one exception is stated plainly rather than buried: the Windows launcher
in launcher/ is a WPF application, and WPF is Microsoft's. Everything it
does is start QEMU with the kernel that is inside it.


